<p><strong>HardPoint Stock</strong> is the part of HardPoint made for phones. Stock clerks use it at the shelf and at the lorry: point the
  camera at a barcode instead of walking back to the counter.</p>

<h2>Opening it</h2>
<ol>
  <li>On a computer, go to <strong>Stock</strong> and scan the QR code at the bottom with the phone, or type <code>yourshop.hardpoint.app/m</code>.</li>
  <li>Sign in with your own HardPoint email and password.</li>
  <li>In Chrome's menu choose <strong>Add to home screen</strong> (Safari: Share › Add to Home Screen) to open it like an app.</li>
</ol>
<p>Scanning with the camera works in Chrome on Android. On other phones, type the barcode or SKU, or pair a Bluetooth barcode scanner, which
  types into the box like a keyboard. Choose your branch at the top.</p>

<h2>Looking something up</h2>
<p>Press <strong>Scan with camera</strong> and point it at the barcode. You see the price, pack prices, stock at every branch, what's on order
  and the latest movements at your branch. Owners, managers and stock clerks also see the cost.</p>

<h2>Counting a stock take</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Someone starts the stock take on a computer (Stock › Stock takes). It then appears under <strong>Count</strong>.</li>
  <li>Scan a product, type how many are there and press <strong>Add to count</strong>. If the same product is on another shelf or in the
    store room, scan it there too and add again. Several people can count at once.</li>
  <li><strong>Set total</strong> replaces the count, for correcting a mistake.</li>
  <li>Scanning a box's barcode asks how many boxes, and counts what's inside.</li>
  <li>The phone doesn't show how many the system expects, so people count what's really there. A manager reviews and approves the stock take on a computer.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Checking in a delivery</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Orders sent to suppliers appear under <strong>Receive</strong>. Open the one that's arrived.</li>
  <li>Scan each item as it comes off the lorry and enter how many; the list shows what's still to come beside what's arrived.
    If everything is plainly there, <strong>Everything still to come has arrived</strong> checks it all in at once.</li>
  <li>Enter the supplier's delivery note number and press <strong>Record delivery</strong>. The goods go into stock, the same as a goods
    received note made on a computer.</li>
  <li>Items that weren't ordered, and transport costs, are added on a computer under Purchasing.</li>
</ul>
